\section{Streamed runs}{

Before writing any output, a run records its configuration in
\code{out/flags_config.rds}: the tests, columns, \code{keep}, reference, buffers,
settings, duplicate options, package version, and each input file with its
size and modification time. A later call into the same \code{out} must match the
record, or it stops and names what differs; \code{resume = FALSE} clears the
directory instead. Sizes and modification times detect a replaced, grown or
touched file; they are not a hash of the contents.

The group stage checkpoints its state after a file or a bucket whenever
five minutes have passed, and at the end of each pass, so an interruption
repeats at most the work since the last checkpoint. The record pass writes
a \code{.done} marker per input file; an interruption there repeats the file in
progress.

A streamed run writes its progress to \code{out/progress.txt}, one line
overwritten on every report: the step, items done and total, seconds
elapsed, and the estimated seconds left in the step and in the run, and the
share of the run done. The estimate for the steps ahead uses per-record
costs measured on a part of the GBIF snapshot, scaled to the speed of the
run so far, so it is rough until the slower steps have started. With
\code{verbose}, the same is reported on the console every minute.
}

\section{Threads and workers}{

\code{n_threads} spreads the exact outlier test over species and the
proximity and point-in-polygon queries of the record tests over records.
Each species and each record is computed on one thread, so the flags do not
depend on the number of threads. Reading the input, the other tests and the
bookkeeping of the group stage run on one thread. Without OpenMP support in
the compiler every query runs on one thread.

\code{workers} runs the record pass of a streamed run over several input files
at once, one file per R process, so reading the input and every record
test run in parallel. Each file is flagged by the same code as in a single
process and writes its own parts and \code{.done} marker, so the output does not
depend on the number of workers. The \code{n_threads} of the run are shared out,
each worker using \code{n_threads \%/\% workers} threads (at least one). Each
worker holds the memory of one record pass, so \code{workers} times the memory
of a single-process record pass has to fit. The group stage runs in the
main process with all \code{n_threads}. Workers load the
installed package, so \code{workers} above 1 stops with an error under
\code{pkgload::load_all()}.

Memory in the record pass is one input chunk, up to \code{part_rows} buffered
output rows and the group-test hits of the current block of 2^20 rows. The
group stage holds two million bucketed rows before writing them (more
above four billion records), the per-species record counts, per-dataset
histograms and raster cell counts of large species, the duplicate keys of
one bucket (at most four million records on average when the input has more
than 256 million), and the records of one species (fewer
than 10,000 for the exact outlier test) or one fossil taxon. Its disk use is
at most 24 bytes per record for \code{duplicate}, plus the coordinates of the
records of the outlier and fossil tests.
}

\section{Tests}{

See \code{\link[=flag_tests]{flag_tests()}} for the layout. Tests equivalent to \href{https://ropensci.github.io/CoordinateCleaner/}{CoordinateCleaner} use
its default buffers: \code{capital} 10 km, \code{centroid} 1 km, \code{institution} 100 m,
\code{gbif_headquarters} 1 km, \code{zero} 0.5 degrees. Proximity tests are closed
balls in WGS84 geodesic distance. Records failing \code{invalid} are given no
spatial flag.

Documented differences to CoordinateCleaner 3.0.1, each verified in the
package tests:
\itemize{
\item CoordinateCleaner approximates each buffer circle by an inscribed polygon
(72 vertices for \code{capital}, \code{centroid}, \code{gbif_headquarters}; 40 for
\code{institution}), so a record between 99.9 \% (\code{capital}, \code{centroid},
\code{gbif_headquarters}) or 99.7 \% (\code{institution}) of the buffer and the
buffer itself is flagged here and may not be there. Everything
CoordinateCleaner flags is flagged here.
\item \code{cc_zero}'s 0.5 degree buffer is interpreted by \href{https://rspatial.github.io/terra/}{terra} as 0.5 metres, so
CoordinateCleaner 3.0.1 flags only exact zeros; this package applies the
documented 0.5 degree radius.
\item \code{country_mismatch} compares the record's code with the ISO alpha-2 code,
the ISO alpha-3 code and the Natural Earth \code{ADM0_A3} code of the country
polygon the point falls in, taking the ISO codes from Natural Earth's
\code{ISO_A2_EH} and \code{ISO_A3_EH} columns, which are filled where \code{ISO_A2} and
\code{ISO_A3} hold \code{-99} (France, Norway, Kosovo); CoordinateCleaner compares
\code{ISO_A3} only, so it flags every record in France and Norway.
Records without a code, or at sea, are flagged as in CoordinateCleaner.
\item \code{outlier} flags the records of outlier cells in the raster approximation
(species of 10,000 records or more, or \code{thinning = TRUE}); in
CoordinateCleaner 3.0.1 that path never flags a record.
\item \code{age_outlier} and \code{spatiotemporal_outlier} draw ages by the same
procedure as \code{cf_age()} and \code{cf_outl()} but from a stream seeded per
taxon, so a single run can differ record by record; when every age range
has zero width the flags are identical.
\item \code{dataset_rounded} leaves datasets below \code{min_unique_ds_size} untested,
where \code{cd_round()} stops with an error; the dataset tests do not flag
records without a dataset key.
\item With the default reference the gazetteers are rebuilt from the original
sources; \code{reference = "CoordinateCleaner"} uses that package's own tables,
and \code{gazetteers} replaces the layer of single tests, as the \code{ref}
arguments of CoordinateCleaner's functions do.
}
